{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Incident 3 (same ticket, after remediation): verifier FAILs without itemizing a criterion\n\nFollowing the remediation the verifier itself prescribes, the three ticket-record-shaped criteria\non `wailsome-unsure-asianlion` were tagged `[operator-attested]` with the concrete evidence named\n(comment titles plus their COMMENT event ids), and the plan review was re-run — PASS, re-signed at\n`cd95a58d7d`. The close was then retried.\n\nIt failed again, with a DIFFERENT and non-actionable verdict:\n\n    Error: completion verification FAILED for cb91-1535-fe3a-47fc - 1 unmet criteria; not closing.\n      - (unspecified): verifier returned FAIL without itemizing the failing criterion.\n\nTicket state at that moment: `status: in_progress`, 4/4 ACs ticked, 2 comments, all events\ncommitted, nothing uncommitted in the tracker.\n\nSo across three runs against substantially the same ticket state the gate produced:\n\n1. 3 unmet criteria, with stated evidence that was demonstrably FALSE (claimed zero comments and\n   status `open` while the store held 12 committed events including two COMMENTs);\n2. (after the prescribed `[operator-attested]` remediation) 1 unmet criterion with NO criterion\n   named and no reason given.\n\nThis is worth weighing in the disposition. Under the \"deliberate review-time snapshot\" hypothesis\nthe run-1 verdict is explicable, but run 3 is not: the tag is the documented escape and it still\nfailed, silently. A verdict that cannot name the criterion it failed leaves an agent with no\nremediation path at all — it is indistinguishable from a structured-output parse failure, and the\nrun's own logs carry `parse_structured: multiple candidates validated ... selecting the LAST`\nwarnings elsewhere in this session, so a transient parse failure surfacing as a FAIL verdict is a\nthird hypothesis worth checking.\n\nSuggested extra check for whoever picks this up: does the verifier distinguish \"the model returned\nFAIL for criterion X\" from \"I could not parse the model's verdict\"? If those collapse into the same\nFAIL, a flaky parse silently blocks closes that should pass."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786585718891639001,"uuid":"db96405d-bb83-4b8d-b8aa-6e0e64f56617"}